description:
FILED s440 (Fable), FOUND BY THE SHAPES CORPUS (Pl/t/shapes/punct-arrays-glob.pl packages each lifted snippet; the snippets share @? and perl shows it, PCL does not).

REPRODUCER (perl 5.40 `e:2 n:3`, PCL `e: n:0`):

    no strict; no warnings;
    { package A; @? = (1,2,3); }
    { package B; print "e:$?[1] n:", scalar(@?), "\n"; }

Same in the statement form (`package A; ... package B; ...`).  @ARGV and %ENV are right (probed: written in A, read in B); so are the punctuation SCALARS presumably (perl forces `$_`, `$0`, `@ARGV`, `%ENV`, `STDIN`, ..., and ALL punctuation names into main:: regardless of the package in scope -- perlvar "Technically, these variables are forced to be in package main").  The #415/#451 punctuation-array emission (the `@?`-family cells) is evidently created per package.

BAR: the reproducer + the ten sibling arrays of #415 (`@! @. @/ @~ @^ @& @% @= @< @>`) + the element/interpolation spellings (`$?[1]`, `"$?[1]"`) across two packages vs perl; the inverse: a NAMED array `@x` in two packages stays two arrays; `@-`/`@+` (already main) unchanged; corpus-diff (emission change -> generation bump + artifacts + A/B); a guard row in Pl/t/punct-array-glob-01.t.  Q7 filler.


FIXED s440c (Fable): the twelve punctuation arrays (the eleven of #415 + the synthesized @#) are RUNTIME-OWNED -- defvar'd and exported from :pcl like @-/@+ -- so every user package reads ONE symbol; Pl::Parser2::_forward_global_decls no longer declares them per package (the %punct bucket is gone; emission change, generation v2-182, artifacts regenerated).  corpus-diff: the 111 IDENTICAL; shapes: only the removed defvar lines in punct-arrays-glob.pl / bareword-handles.pl; lib A/B 22 SAME.  Guard: Pl/t/punct-array-glob-01.t (+3 rows: block form with all eleven siblings, statement form through three packages, the NAMED-array inverse).  ir-spec 2b "Punctuation names are main:: everywhere".  Runtime+emission change -> the sweep runs with the s441 batch.
